# Issue → execution

When the user gives an idea, a spec, or says "add an issue / branch / do it all" — do not write code before the spec is agreed. Create the issue first, then follow its items exactly.

## 1. Spec first, then the issue

- If they ask only for a plan / spec — text only, no commits or files.
- The spec must lock: goal, behavior, out of scope, acceptance, work order.
- Work order always includes: issue, branch `issue-{N}-{slug}`, versions if a release is needed, tests, docker when the image changes, commit, push, PR, release tag only after merge to `main`.
- Create the issue with `gh issue create` (or GitHub MCP). Search for duplicates first.
- Title: one sentence of what the user gets. Example: `Topics Visibility: rank the repository on its GitHub topics`.
- Issue body is the compressed spec:

```markdown
## Summary
<why>

## Behavior
- <visible behavior and constraints>

## Out of scope
- <what we will not do>

## Acceptance
- <how to verify>
```

## 2. Execution

Once the issue exists and it should be implemented — follow only the checklist in `issue-execution.mdc` (branch, code, 95% coverage, ITs, UI, commit/push, PR, CI, Codecov, restart Docker, leave the PR open).

## 3. After merge

- Push a `vX.Y.Z` release tag only from `main` after green CI, and only when the user asks for a release.
- The tag starts `.github/workflows/release.yml` (GHCR + GitHub Release).
